# Line endings — one standard, shared verbatim by every Facturatix repository.
#
# The repository stores LF, and every checkout gets LF too: Windows, macOS and Linux alike,
# whatever `core.autocrlf` or `core.eol` says on the machine. Attributes take precedence over local
# configuration, so cloning is enough and nobody has to configure anything.
#
# Why LF everywhere rather than each platform's "native" ending: the tools that read these files
# do not agree on what native means. Prettier's default (`endOfLine: lf`) and the ESLint rule that
# enforces it reject every line of a CRLF checkout with `Delete ␍`; a shell script or Dockerfile
# with a CR before the newline fails inside a Linux container looking for `/bin/sh\r`; the .NET
# formatter follows `.editorconfig`, which says LF. A checkout that is CRLF on one machine and LF
# on another is one where the same command passes on one and fails on the other. Every editor and
# toolchain used here — Visual Studio, Rider, VS Code, the .NET SDK, Node, PowerShell — reads and
# writes LF without complaint. The one Windows format that genuinely needs CRLF is cmd.exe batch,
# listed below.
#
# Every repository already held LF in its index when `eol=lf` was added, so no committed file
# changed. A working tree cloned before this change may still hold CRLF; refresh it once, on a
# clean tree (stash or commit first):
#     git rm --cached -r . && git reset --hard
#
# `.editorconfig` is the other half: it makes editors save and create files with LF, so what the
# editor writes and what git checks out never disagree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ── Must be LF even if the default above ever changes ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# These are read by a Linux kernel inside a container or on a CI runner. A CR before the newline
# makes it look for an interpreter literally named `/bin/sh\r`, and the build fails with a message
# that names neither the file nor the reason.
*.sh              text eol=lf
Dockerfile        text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile      text eol=lf
.dockerignore     text eol=lf

# ── The one format that needs CRLF: cmd.exe batch files ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# cmd.exe reads a batch file line by line while executing it and mis-parses labels and multi-line
# blocks that end in a bare LF. Nothing else on Windows has this constraint; PowerShell does not.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# ── Generated lockfiles: regenerate, never hand-merge ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A line-by-line merge of `pnpm-lock.yaml` can produce a file that looks valid and is not: when two
# branches independently add the same package version, git keeps both blocks and pnpm fails with
# ERR_PNPM_BROKEN_LOCKFILE (duplicated mapping key) — which is how facturatix-landing PR #23 showed
# as mergeable while broken. `merge=binary` keeps the diff readable but stops git from inventing a
# merge: both sides touching the lockfile is a conflict, resolved by
#     git checkout --ours pnpm-lock.yaml && pnpm install
# Dependabot rebases on its own when this happens, and CI revalidates the branch on the real base.
pnpm-lock.yaml    text eol=lf merge=binary linguist-generated=true
package-lock.json text eol=lf merge=binary linguist-generated=true

# ── Explicit text, so one stray control byte cannot turn a source file binary ─────────────────
#
# `text=auto` decides by content: a single NUL anywhere in the first 8 KB makes git call the file
# binary, stop diffing it and skip normalization entirely. Naming the source extensions means a
# code file is always treated as code, and the damage shows up as a diff instead of hiding in one.
*.cs     text diff=csharp
*.ts     text
*.tsx    text
*.mts    text
*.cts    text
*.js     text
*.mjs    text
*.cjs    text
*.jsx    text
*.json   text
*.jsonc  text
*.css    text
*.scss   text
*.html   text
*.md     text
*.mdx    text
*.yml    text
*.yaml   text
*.toml   text
*.sql    text
*.ps1    text
*.csproj text
*.props  text
*.targets text
*.slnx   text
*.svg    text
*.txt    text
*.env*   text

